---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "./_privacy/privacy.scss";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy"
  pageTitle="Crona privacy"
  description="Read Crona's privacy policy for app telemetry, consent, diagnostics, and what stays local on your device."
>
  <section class="container section">
    <div class="page-header flow measure">
      <p class="eyebrow">Privacy policy</p>
      <h1>Crona privacy policy</h1>
      <p class="privacy-date">Effective date: May 16, 2026</p>
      <p>
        This page explains what Crona collects, what stays on your device, how telemetry consent works, and how to
        change the setting in the app.
      </p>
    </div>

    <nav class="privacy-toc page-body" aria-label="Privacy policy sections">
      <a href="#scope">Scope</a>
      <a href="#collected">What Crona collects</a>
      <a href="#use">How Crona uses information</a>
      <a href="#sharing">How Crona shares information</a>
      <a href="#retention">Retention</a>
      <a href="#choices">Your choices</a>
      <a href="#children">Children's privacy</a>
      <a href="#changes">Changes to this policy</a>
      <a href="#contact">Contact</a>
    </nav>

    <div class="privacy-body measure flow">
      <section id="scope" class="privacy-section">
        <h2>1. Scope</h2>
        <p>
          This policy covers the Crona app and the crona.work website. It explains what Crona collects, what stays
          local, and how telemetry consent works.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="collected" class="privacy-section">
        <h2>2. What Crona collects</h2>
        <p>
          Crona keeps your work data on your device. Issues, habits, sessions, check-ins, exports, calendar content,
          notes, and support bundle contents are not sent as telemetry.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you turn telemetry on, Crona can send a small set of app signals: startup and shutdown events, release
          channel details, handled errors, and panic errors. Telemetry also includes a few context fields such as the
          app name and version, environment mode, running release channel, whether the build is beta, and basic
          platform information like operating system and architecture.
        </p>
        <p>
          Crona also stores a local anonymous identifier so telemetry can stay consistent across launches without asking
          for your name or email.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="use" class="privacy-section">
        <h2>3. How Crona uses information</h2>
        <p>
          Crona uses telemetry to understand crashes, startup behavior, and release channel usage. Error reports are
          sanitized before they are sent so they can help with debugging without including your work content or file
          paths.
        </p>
        <p>
          The app uses this information to keep the current release line healthy, separate stable and beta traffic, and
          make support easier when something goes wrong.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="sharing" class="privacy-section">
        <h2>4. How Crona shares information</h2>
        <p>
          When telemetry is enabled, Crona sends analytics and error data to its telemetry provider for processing. It
          does not use this information for advertising or sell it to third parties.
        </p>
        <p>
          Bundled release installs include telemetry defaults, but they still wait for your consent before any data is
          sent.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="retention" class="privacy-section">
        <h2>5. Retention</h2>
        <p>
          Your work data stays local until you remove it from your device. Telemetry data is kept only as long as needed
          to operate and troubleshoot Crona through the telemetry provider and local logs.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="choices" class="privacy-section">
        <h2>6. Your choices</h2>
        <p>
          Usage telemetry and error reporting are both off until onboarding is complete and you enable them in the app.
          You can turn one on without the other if you want crash reporting without broader usage tracking.
        </p>
        <p>
          Open the in-app <strong>Privacy &amp; Diagnostics</strong> pane to review or change the setting at any time.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="children" class="privacy-section">
        <h2>7. Children's privacy</h2>
        <p>
          Crona is not directed to children under 13, and Crona does not knowingly collect personal information from
          children under 13.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="changes" class="privacy-section">
        <h2>8. Changes to this policy</h2>
        <p>
          If Crona changes its telemetry or privacy practices, this page will be updated so the current behavior stays
          documented here.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="contact" class="privacy-section">
        <h2>9. Contact</h2>
        <p>
          If you have a privacy question, use the support page or open a GitHub issue so it can be reviewed with the
          rest of the project.
        </p>
      </section>

      <p>
        <a class="button" href="/support/">Open support</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
